WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.000
A newborn ferret's eyes have not opened yet.

2
00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.620
Even so, its retina is firing.

3
00:00:09.667 --> 00:00:13.707
Its ganglion cells fire bursts that sweep across the tissue as waves.

4
00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:16.207
Nothing is being seen.

5
00:00:17.127 --> 00:00:18.167
Why send this to the brain?

6
00:00:19.217 --> 00:00:21.037
The brain is still wiring itself.

7
00:00:22.057 --> 00:00:24.097
Molecules have drawn only a rough first map.

8
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:28.437
In the thalamus, the two eyes' axons at first overlap.

9
00:00:29.417 --> 00:00:30.417
Later they separate.

10
00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:36.160
Two cells side by side in one eye sit at almost the same point on the gradients.

11
00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:41.720
So does a cell at the matching point in the other eye, though molecular cues also

12
00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.300
differ somewhat between the eyes.

13
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:48.300
It still has to end up in a different territory, and the cues alone do not finish

14
00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:49.300
the job.

15
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.203
Timing can sort them.

16
00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:55.903
Watch a wave cross the retina at about 100 micrometres a second.

17
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:59.233
Two cells this close fire almost together.

18
00:01:00.193 --> 00:01:01.933
Their bursts overlap almost completely.

19
00:01:03.033 --> 00:01:07.513
A cell a millimetre farther along the wave's path fires ten seconds later.

20
00:01:08.433 --> 00:01:09.653
Their bursts do not overlap.

21
00:01:10.683 --> 00:01:14.643
Now predict. What about a cell at the same point in the other eye?

22
00:01:21.016 --> 00:01:25.956
Its waves are not timed to this eye's, so it matches only by chance, a few percent

23
00:01:25.956 --> 00:01:26.956
of the time.

24
00:01:27.566 --> 00:01:31.226
But waves start in many places and run in varying directions.

25
00:01:32.316 --> 00:01:37.156
So position is carried not by one cell's timing, but by how closely two cells'

26
00:01:37.156 --> 00:01:39.176
firing matches, over many waves.

27
00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.240
How could a target cell use that match?

28
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.700
In 1949, the psychologist Donald Hebb proposed a rule.

29
00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:53.113
In paraphrase: when cell A repeatedly takes part in firing cell B, A becomes more

30
00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:54.553
effective at firing B.

31
00:01:55.566 --> 00:02:00.586
Take one thalamic cell with inputs from both eyes, each firing in its own waves.

32
00:02:01.506 --> 00:02:03.646
Predict: what happens to the right eye's inputs?

33
00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.160
If they are strong enough to fire the cell at their own times, they strengthen too.

34
00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:18.100
Hebb's rule only strengthens, so the cell stays binocular.

35
00:02:19.183 --> 00:02:22.003
Segregation needs a second half: competition.

36
00:02:23.183 --> 00:02:25.403
Picture the inputs sharing a fixed budget.

37
00:02:26.283 --> 00:02:30.063
Each round, both eyes grow, then the same amount is taken back from each.

38
00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:34.563
The eye that starts slightly ahead gains more than it gives back.

39
00:02:35.023 --> 00:02:36.483
So it pulls further ahead.

40
00:02:37.466 --> 00:02:41.226
In this toy model, the other eye's inputs are pushed out of that cell.

41
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:46.320
Rescale both eyes in proportion instead, and the split never changes.

42
00:02:47.220 --> 00:02:48.320
The kind of sharing matters.

43
00:02:49.383 --> 00:02:51.323
Takeaway: timing is the address.

44
00:02:52.283 --> 00:02:56.763
A cell keeps inputs that fire in step with its strong inputs, and they crowd out the

45
00:02:56.763 --> 00:02:57.003
rest.
